iation like  d5 cd5 bishop f5 there's going to be  enough resources for white to uh to win  the pawn and actually i'm realizing  right here that white can immediately  take  after all the trades there's queen a4 in  the end  so  black has to be a bit careful in these  lines  um one thing i will say  and this is something that uh  actually i i saw earlier today anna  crowling was doing a stream  with her dad  who's a well-known grandmaster  and there's a variation here pawn b5  uh known as a ballon gambit her dad's  name is actually isbellin so it's named  after him  and it was super cool lecture i think  she's planning to put the video on  youtube  but uh this is a tricky sideline which  if white's not prepared for it can be  very dangerous i studied this a long  time ago and i would have known to play  the um  basically the so-called refutation d3  i don't want to go too far down the  rabbit hole but um  it is fun sometimes to to study these  these off-beat  and sharp variations  so after knight three my opponent didn't  play e4  played by far the most common move  knight c6  and  here i played a move which has kind of  been a pet line of mine  over the many years  actually if we look in the database  the move i played is the fourth most  popular move  more popular is g3 e3 e4  but my choice is pawn a3  and the reason why i like this move  especially against lesser experienced  players  is  it prevents the bishop from developing  so actively  and there's a few potential traps here  like if the bishop does develop to c5  white can start with e3  and the media thread is to play b4 and  try and trap the bishop so if black is  really not careful this could  potentially happen  but i've had a decent number of games  over the years once in a tournament  probably a few times online  where my opponents play a5  and this allows for a center fork trick  after knight takes e5 i take c5 and d4  and i'm actually curious here  um i've had this position a few times on  each s  and uh the one tournament game i had  here my opponent played bishop d6 and  then c5 is very strong  and white already has a  very significant advantage  engine approved  so  black does have to be careful developing  the bishop too early  and i prevented bishop to b4 so it's a  bit of a question  how black should complete development  and my opponent played  one of the most natural moves pawn d5  now this move is a little bit risky to  play  because it's going into an open sicilian  down a tempo  and because i have the extra tempo  there's more room to play aggressively  which we're going to see  very soon how the game unfolds and how  black falls under major pressure  i will say the safer approach is to play  g6  and this is a more kind of slow  positional game  i would have played g3 bishop g7 bishop  d2  castling include d3  and play a structure like this which is  way more of a kind of standard english  position  but uh in the game my opponent played d5  and  we got  what we can kind of call a reverse  taimanov i'm not sure if lee chest has a  name for this  calls it king's english variation four  knights korchnoid line and the reason  for playing queen c2 it's a very  flexible move in some cases i want to  target the knight in combination with  pawn e3 and bishop e5 which we're going  to see  and i also control this diagonal which  could be relevant in in some cases  for example there's a line  bishop to e7 pawn e3  castling and then after takes takes  really nice move bishop d3  and in the masters database this has  been played good number of times  white trends not only bishop takes h7  but also bishop e4  in combination with b4 bishop b2 there's  a lot of ways to pressure black center  from very early on  so in the game  my opponent played very natural move  bishop e6  and one of the issues for black in this  sort of position is  natural moves  tend to fall into trouble very quickly  because after e3  bishop e7  i pounce with bishop to b5  and now we can see that black already  has a lot of issues to deal with  mainly the fact that the pawn is  undefended  and there's a lot of pressure against  black setup and it's not entirely clear  how to keep everything defended  a move like f6 which does defend the  pawn walks into d4 and white's just  continuing to throw punches here if pawn  takes d4 then knight can take back the  bishops hit knights hits  bishop d7 fails for knight d5  so we can see how things go downhill for  black very quickly so after bishop to b5  my opponent played bishop f6  which  looks like a natural move it's one  attempt to try and keep things defended  but here i go for 94  and okay this is where i'll hide the  notation don't want to spoil the rest of  the game  but knight e4 it's a nice multi-purpose  move  not only unleashing the queen  and mounting even more pressure against  the pin knight but also having ideas of  knight to c5  so in the event of queen d7  trying to keep everything defended  this just fails miserably to knight c5  queens attacked if the queen moves back  there's some nice tactics here knight  takes b7  queen takes takes and it's a fork skewer  of goodness and uh likes winning a ton  of material  so  my opponent took a bit of a think after  a knight e4  and realized that there's not a great  way to defend the knights  although maybe it should have been uh  like a move like 97 should have been  considered  but my opponent decided to castle  which i think in some sense is a decent  practical decision  uh trying to treat this as a gambit  and uh  baiting me to take a pawn and hoping for  some compensation  now i did go ahead i took on on c6 went  up a pawn  and now it's really up to black to try  and fight back in this position  so my opponent played rookie 8  and i castled  and right after i castled i realized  that i missed my opponent's next move  which my opponent played  relatively quickly is bishop to g4  and if i had seen this coming maybe i  would have played something different  like maybe h3 would be worth it  but the thing after bishop g4 is  black wants to damage my king side  structure  and there's not a convenient way of  defending the knights or moving the  knights  so  this is where i probably took my longest  think of the game  uh just trying to figure out how to deal  with  this potential threat  and i came to the conclusion that i'd be  okay with the transformation of the  structure  because there's not an easy way for  black to exploit the king  and i decided to play pawn to b4  now i do want to address the move knight  takes f6 which looks very natural i  think a lot of a lot of people watching  would play this move  but this only helps black because after  knight takes  there's potential for pawn e4  and rookie six is coming and it's a  little bit uh  murkier in in this position  so pawn b4 it's an attempt to complete  development  i realize my bishop it's my worst place  piece i wanted to turn it into something  useful  and uh i did allow bishop takes off  three g takes f3  and now rook to e6  so  there's still a little bit of uh of  concern given black has initiative  there's a potential of the rook lift  but after queen to c2 it still takes  time for black to try and organize an  attack so my opponent played bishop h4  one of only ways to make way for the  rook  and here i play knight g3  a very safe move  covering the g file  unleashing the queen along the diagonal  opponent pins and knight  and now i play queen e4 so centralizing  the queen  um  here i'm beginning to find some nice  harmony with the pieces the queen is  very useful in e4  pressuring a lot of targets in black's  position  my bishop is ready to develop rook is  ready to come in on top of all of this  i'm up a pawn and my king is very safe  and perhaps because of like all these  reasons that  the game is beginning to clearly go in  my favor  my opponent made a terrible wonder here  played the move queen d7 completely  missing that the bishop is now no longer  defended  i happily scooped up the bishop  i'm sure my opponent would have loved to  have a few extra moves to play this and  this and this and this  but just not enough time  i took on each four  rook h6 was played  i slide over would be very happy with a  queen trade  my opponent  continued the downward spiral  played queen g6  and allowed a very feel-good fork on f5  hitting the queen the rook and  threatening mate  so essentially a triple fork  and here's where uh black resigned  now black could try and defend with a  move like queen g6 or rook g6 but  ever which way black tries i'm gonna be  up a rook in the end  and a pawn so  not much hope left  so nine to five was a nice way of  finishing up my second round game  and this gave me a two out of two start  to the tournament  and if we reflect on this game it was  just a matter of getting an opening  where my opponent was perhaps a bit less  familiar  a bit less comfortable  didn't quite know how to deal with the  early pressure  uh which can be very typical uh from the  english opening  sometimes english has a reputation of  being more slow and maneuvering  but in a more open position like this  especially reverse open sicilian we can  see that black does have to be very  careful  and  maybe it would be worth my opponent  exploring some of the theoretical lines  a better option is to play bishop e7  and castle quickly  before  uh entering the the trouble lines with  the pin against the knight so uh i was  feeling pretty good this uh  set me up pretty well for round three  which i will cover in the next video  so hope you guys enjoyed the game  as always if you have questions leave  them below  i will be reading the comments  if you're enjoying the the series so far  feel free to like and subscribe  it does help the channel grow  and i'll see you in the next video

